  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nGuidance consensus remains consistent in northwesterly flow \nprevailing over the Middle Mississippi Valley as the upper-level \nridge holds over the western CONUS through the extended forecast \nrange. With flow lower in the atmosphere persisting generally out of \nthe northeast, the thermal ridge responsible for our early week heat \nwill remain focused over the Great Plains. This pattern will keep \nlocal temperatures seasonable through the weekend. \n\nA strong shortwave is still seen in the majority of guidance diving \nsouth-southeastward across the Midwest late Thursday through \nSaturday, settling into the Ohio Valley on Sunday. This wave and \nit's associated surface system will bring rain chances to the region \nto end the week. Phasing of the wave varies enough among guidance to \nproduce widely different evolutions of the surface system, leading \nto low confidence in specifics on timing and placement of rainfall. \nThere at least has been some consistency in the CWA being in the \nwarm sector of the surface system Thursday nigh into Friday, \nfavoring the greatest chances for rainfall through that stretch. \nSome guidance has a slower shortwave, which would then linger rain \nchances through Saturday and even possibly Sunday, whereas a faster \nwave would confine rain chances to late Thursday into Friday. The \ncurrent forecast splits the difference, with a bulk of the rain \nchances (60%) Thursday night into Friday, with low chances (20-30%) \nlingering into Saturday.",
